Hmm wait — careful: `cancelToken.onCancel` — if the token was already cancelled... fine. Also the CancelToken contract: after the ladder's reset(), a REPLAYED attempt gets the SAME token (live again). Each attempt registers its own onCancel listener — the one-shot listener caveat: listeners registered after reset fire on next cancel ✓.

Careful subtlety: on watchdog fire, token cancels → the pending attempt 1's `Future.any` completes via onCancel → pushes aborted error → ladder replays (watchdog class) → resets token → attempt 2 registers a NEW onCancel listener. Attempt 2's warmup future still has `silence - elapsed` remaining... wait no — each `call` invocation creates a FRESH `Future.delayed(silence)` — so attempt 2 waits the FULL silence again! That breaks my AC1 math: silence 150ms, window 120ms → attempt 1 killed at 120ms; attempt 2 waits ANOTHER 150ms → killed at 240ms; budget 2 → terminal. The run DIES. ✗.

The real llama.cpp warmup: the SERVER is warming; the second request arrives mid-warmup and its first byte comes when the warmup completes — from the second request's perspective it waits the REMAING warmup (roughly). To model deterministically: a shared warmup deadline: `final readyAt = DateTime.now().add(silence)` fixed at provider construction; each attempt waits `max(0, readyAt - now)` then streams. That models "the endpoint completes its warmup at a fixed wall-clock moment" — deterministic and matches AC1 (35-min warmup then responds).

```dart
class _WarmingUpProvider {
  _WarmingUpProvider(this.silence);
  final Duration silence;
  late final DateTime readyAt = DateTime.now().add(silence);
  int calls = 0;
  ...
  final remaining = readyAt.difference(DateTime.now());
  if (remaining > Duration.zero) {
    await Future.any([Future<void>.delayed(remaining), cancelToken?.onCancel ?? ...]);
  }
  if (cancelled) push aborted else push textTurn('warmed up') + end.
}
```

